**1. Install Base System Dependencies**
Debian 13 (Trixie) requires core build tools and network libraries installed via the package manager.

bash
apt update
apt install -y git build-essential cmake libcurl4-openssl-dev zlib1g-dev

**2. Install the Latest Rust Toolchain**
Debian's default `rustc` package (1.85.1) is too old for CCExtractor's 1.87.0 minimum requirement. The toolchain must be installed directly via the official `rustup` script.

bash
curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -sSf https://sh.rustup.rs | sh -s -- -y
source "$HOME/.cargo/env"

**3. Compile GPAC from Source**
The `libgpac-dev` package is obsolete in Debian 13 repositories. The framework must be compiled manually from GitHub so CCExtractor can process MP4 containers.

bash
cd ~
git clone https://github.com/gpac/gpac.git
cd gpac
./configure
make -j$(nproc)
make install
ldconfig

**4. Compile CCExtractor from Source**
With all dependencies in place, CCExtractor is cloned and compiled using CMake pointed at its `src/` directory.

bash
cd ~
git clone https://github.com/CCExtractor/ccextractor.git
cd ccextractor
mkdir build && cd build
cmake ../src/
make -j$(nproc)

**5. Install the Binary**
The compiled executable is moved to the system path so applications like Tdarr can access it globally.

bash
cp ccextractor /usr/local/bin/